      "answer_id": "A4",
      "answer": "Finasteride treats hair loss by inhibiting **SRD5A2 (steroid 5-alpha-reductase type 2)**, the enzyme that converts **testosterone to dihydrotestosterone (DHT)**.  \n\nIn this context, the key biological narrative is:\n\n- **SRD5A2** is part of **androgen biosynthesis**.\n- Finasteride blocks SRD5A2 activity, which **reduces DHT production**.\n- Because **DHT is a major androgen driving hair follicle miniaturization in androgenetic alopecia**, lowering DHT helps **slow or reverse hair loss**.\n- The graph also lists **AR (androgen receptor)** as a targeted gene, which fits the broader androgen-signaling context, but the central, established mechanism is **DHT suppression via SRD5A2 inhibition**.\n\nSo, in hair-loss treatment, finasteride works mainly as a **5-alpha-reductase inhibitor that lowers scalp/systemic DHT, thereby reducing androgen-driven follicle miniaturization**.\n\nThe supplied context does not provide additional hair-specific mechanistic details beyond this androgen/DHT pathway."
